---
import { releaseEvidence } from "../lib/evidence";
import { artifactHref } from "../lib/urls";

const { trialeval: te, trialdev: td } = releaseEvidence();
const integer = (value: number) => value.toLocaleString("en-US");
const fixed = (value: number, digits: number) => value.toFixed(digits);
const percent = (value: number) => `${(100 * value).toFixed(0)}%`;
const money = (value: number) => `${value < 0 ? "−" : ""}$${Math.abs(value).toFixed(1)}M`;
const costs = new Map(te.policy_public.naive_costs.map(row => [row.regime_cell_id, row]));
const record = (name: string) => artifactHref(`release-evidence/${name}`);
const effect = te.realism.effect_distribution;
---
<section id="release-evidence" aria-labelledby="release-evidence-heading">
  <h2 id="release-evidence-heading">Release evidence</h2>
  <p>Every number in this section is read from the <a href={record("release-evidence.json")}>release evidence record</a>, which the <a href={record("RELEASE_FACTS.md")}>release facts</a> and the <a href={record("cells.md")}>cell cards</a> restate. A series' cells share each base world, and the evidence contexts of a world share its participants, so the unit of evidence is the independent base world rather than the task.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Release composition and qualification">
    <table>
      <caption>Release composition and qualification</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Benchmark and split</th><th scope="col">Cells</th><th scope="col">Independent worlds</th><th scope="col">Tasks</th><th scope="col">Qualification</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">TrialEval, public</th><td>{te.public.regime_cells} ({te.public.a2_cells} A2, {te.public.trap_cells} traps)</td><td>{te.public.independent_base_worlds} ({te.public.series} series × {te.public.laws_per_series} laws)</td><td>{integer(te.public.tasks)}</td><td>{te.public.accepted ? "Accepted" : "Not accepted"}, {te.public.failed_cells} failed cells</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">TrialEval, withheld</th><td>{te.withheld.regime_cells - te.withheld.held_out_cells} public + {te.withheld.held_out_cells} held out ({te.withheld.a2_cells} A2, {te.withheld.trap_cells} traps)</td><td>{te.withheld.independent_base_worlds} ({te.withheld.series} series × {te.withheld.laws_per_series} laws)</td><td>{integer(te.withheld.tasks)}</td><td>{te.withheld.accepted ? "Accepted" : "Not accepted"}, {te.withheld.failed_cells} failed cells</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">TrialDev, public</th><td>—</td><td>{integer(td.public.programmes)} programmes</td><td>{integer(td.public.tasks)}</td><td>{td.public.accepted ? "Accepted" : "Not accepted"}, {td.public.failed} failed</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">TrialDev, withheld</th><td>—</td><td>{integer(td.withheld.programmes)} programmes</td><td>{integer(td.withheld.tasks)}</td><td>{td.withheld.accepted ? "Accepted" : "Not accepted"}, {td.withheld.failed} failed</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The public and withheld TrialEval splits share {te.base_worlds_shared_between_splits} base worlds. Recovery was measured on {integer(te.recovery.prior_drawn_worlds_per_cell)} prior-drawn and {integer(te.recovery.coverage_worlds_per_cell)} coverage worlds per cell. In the public split, {te.public.pair_parity.identical_pairs} of {te.public.pair_parity.pairs} trap–twin pairs present identical documents once identifiers, dates and data-dependent times are masked.</p>

  <h3>The traps change decisions</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Excluded analysis in the public trap cells">
    <table>
      <caption>The excluded analysis in the public trap cells, priced over {integer(te.policy_public.worlds_per_cell)} prior-drawn worlds per cell</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Trap cell</th><th scope="col">Interval width ÷ credited</th><th scope="col">Interval coverage</th><th scope="col">Squared error ÷ credited</th><th scope="col">Posterior-expected regret per answer</th><th scope="col">Consequential errors</th><th scope="col">Regret at the true effect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{te.consequence.map(row => { const cost = costs.get(row.regime_cell_id); return <tr><th scope="row">{row.regime_cell_id}</th><td>{fixed(row.width_ratio, 2)}</td><td>{fixed(row.shortcut_coverage, 2)}</td><td>{fixed(row.loss_ratio, 2)}</td><td>{cost ? `${money(cost.regret_musd)} ± ${fixed(cost.regret_se_musd, 1)}` : "—"}</td><td>{cost ? percent(cost.consequential_share) : "—"}</td><td>{cost ? money(cost.regret_at_truth_musd) : "—"}</td></tr>; })}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Regret at the true effect is valued at each prior-drawn world's law target over {integer(te.policy_public.worlds_per_cell)} worlds per cell, so it averages over the disclosed effect prior.</p>

  <h3>Correcting in the twin also changes decisions</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The trap's correction applied in the public twins">
    <table>
      <caption>The trap's correction applied in each public A2 twin</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Twin cell</th><th scope="col">Consequential errors</th><th scope="col">Mean regret per answer</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{te.over_corrections.map(row => <tr><th scope="row">{row.regime_cell_id}</th><td>{percent(row.consequential_share)}</td><td>{money(row.mean_regret_musd)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <slot />

  {td.panel && <p>In the public TrialDev main selection the reference policy reaches {fixed(td.panel.public.reference_normalised[0], 3)} (95% interval {fixed(td.panel.public.reference_normalised[1], 3)}–{fixed(td.panel.public.reference_normalised[2], 3)}) of the attainable oracle's value and beats stopping at once at z = {fixed(td.panel.public.reference_minus_stop_z, 1)}.</p>}

  <h3>Realism against published trials</h3>
  <p>Each quantity is compared only with evidence disjoint from what calibrated it: the half of the matched registry cohort that did not calibrate it, or published exemplar trials that did not. Of {te.realism.cells} compared quantities: inside the disjoint range {te.realism.status_counts.agrees ?? 0} · within a stated tolerance {te.realism.status_counts.within_tolerance ?? 0} · departure {te.realism.departures}. A value outside its band departs only when its whole 95% interval (value ± 1.96 between-world standard errors) lies outside the band too. The <a href={record("realism/realism_linkage.md")}>linkage report</a> names the calibration source, the exemplar trials and their locators for every comparison.</p>
  <figure><img src={record("realism/realism_scorecard.svg")} alt="Realism scorecard: each series by compared quantity, marked by status." /><figcaption>Each series by compared quantity, marked by status.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure><img src={record("realism/realism_position.svg")} alt="Each synthetic value placed within its range from published exemplar trials, where the range spans zero to one." /><figcaption>Each synthetic value placed against its exemplar range (0 and 1 mark the range ends).</figcaption></figure>
  {effect && <figure><img src={record("realism/realism_effect_qq.svg")} alt={`Quantiles of credited-analysis z statistics against the disclosed effect prior over ${integer(effect.worlds)} prior-drawn worlds.`} /><figcaption>Credited-analysis z statistics against the disclosed effect prior over {integer(effect.worlds)} prior-drawn worlds; {fixed(effect.share_beyond_196, 3)} lie beyond 1.96.</figcaption></figure>}
</section>
